## 1. Introduction
The photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction into useful chemicals, a desirable process in manual photosynthesis, attracted widespread attention for producing carbon fuels and relieving climate change [1–3]. It was essential of developing efficient photocatalysts to improve the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction performance for practical applications [4–6]. Over the past decade, graphitic carbon nitride $(\mathsf{g}\mathsf{-}C_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4})$ attracted enormous interest [7–11]. However, the application of bulk $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ (BCN) was limited by poor conductivity, unsatisfactory stabilities, and higher carrier recombination [12–14]. Constructing heterojunctions could induce the rapid separation of charge carriers to improve photocatalytic activity [15–17].
Benefiting from unique physicochemical characteristics, metal– organic frameworks (MOFs) emerged as a potential photocatalyst for the synergistic effect of their metal centers and organic linkages [18]. Especially, MOFs with hollow structures exhibited larger specific surface areas to provide more surface-active sites [19,20]. What is more, the MOFs with hollow structures could improve light absorption owing to the multiple reflections within the catalyst [21,22]. Apart from the above merits, photocatalysts based on MOFs exhibited periodic structures, adjustable composition, and controllable morphology. Recently, indium-oxide $\left(\mathrm{{In}}_{2}{\mathrm{{O}}}_{3}\right)$ had received particular attention as a potential catalyst for its excellent chemical stability and suitable reduction potential in the field of catalysis [23]. Lou et al. designed $Z_{\mathrm{{nln}_{2}{S_{4}}}}$ assembling on both inner and outer surfaces of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ hierarchical tubular to fabricate sandwich-like heterojunctions for efficient ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction [24]. Dai et al. demonstrated the promotion of special MOFsassisted heterostructure on charge transfer by constructing carbon-coated hollow $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ [25]. Leung et al. constructed Zscheme heterojunctions by combining the MIL-68 with AgCl for promoting the ability of tetracycline photooxidation and Cr (VI) photoreduction [26]. Therefore, it was desirable to utilize $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ fully encapsulated hollow MOFs-assisted $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ to explore the benefits of their unique structures in photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction. However, the MOFs-assisted $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ fully encapsulated by $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction has not been reported.
Herein, we demonstrated a MOFs-assisted thermal deposition method to construct Z-scheme CN/INO heterojunctions which exhibited a fully encapsulated hollow tubular structure. Firstly, hexagonal In-MIL-68 prism precursors were synthesized through a facile oil bath reaction. Next, CNNS was fixed on the surface of the hexagonal In-MIL-68 prism via one-step thermal deposition. The photocatalytic functions of porous CNNS and hexagonal $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ were integrated into heterojunction. Especially, the ligands of In-MIL-68 decomposed and released large amounts of gas, which could promote the formation of hierarchical porous architectures. The unique structure could assist in the separation and migration of photogenerated charges. The enlarged surface area exposed plentiful reactive sites for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption and surface reactions. The photoactivity was appraised by photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction under visible-light irradiation $400\ \mathrm{nm}<\lambda<780\ \mathrm{nm})$ , respectively. What is more, the work functions and charge difference density plot of samples were calculated by the density functional theory (DFT) to illustrate the charge transfer of the sample surface in theory. To further demonstrate the photogenerated carriers transfer mechanism within a sample, Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM) was carried out on the sample. The in-situ Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) was measured to deeply investigate the possible reaction pathway of reducing ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ .
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Material synthesis
Melamine $(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}\mathsf{N}_{6},\mathsf{\Omega}>99\mathrm{\Omega}\%$ , $\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}\mathrm{xH}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(>99.9\mathrm{~\%~})$ , 1,4 benzene dicarboxylic acid $(>99\%)$ , and N, N dimethylformamide (DMF, $>99.8~\%$ were purchased from Aladdin Chemical Reagent Co., ltd. The ethanol $(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{5}0\mathsf{H},>95\ \%$ were purchased from Sinopharm Co., ltd. All reagents used were analytical reagent which did not need further purification.
## 2.2. Synthesis of samples
Preparation of BCN: The BCN was synthesized by the thermal polymerization method which was commonly used in the literature [26,27].
Preparation of In-MIL-68: The hexagonal In-MIL-68 was prepared according to the previous literature with modifications [28,29]. Typically, $_{0.6\ {\ g}}$ of $\ln(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}$ and $0.6\ \mathrm{g}$ of 1,4 benzene dicarboxylic acid were added to $25~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF. After completed dissolution, the above two solutions were thoroughly mixed. Subsequently, the mixed solution was heated at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ with an oil bath. When the temperature returned to room temperature, the white In-MIL-68 was obtained by the process of filtration and washing with ethanol.
Preparation of hexagonal $I n_{2}O_{3}$ microtubes: The hexagonal $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ microtubes were prepared by heating In-MIL-68 prisms in the air with programmed warming treatment. Typically, the In-MIL-68 prisms were heated in air at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ , and then further annealed at $500^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for another $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
Preparation of hierarchical porous CN/INO heterostructures: To synthesize the hierarchical porous CN/INO heterostructures, the BCN was first mixed with In-MIL-68 prisms mechanically and ground together for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The ratios of BCN to In-MIL-68 were 3:1, 2:1, and 1:1, respectively. Then, the mixed prisms were annealed with the same method of hexagonal $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ microtubes. For simplicity, as-prepared products are labeled as CN/INO-3, $\mathsf{C N}/$ INO-2, and CN/INO-1, respectively. The schematic diagram of the synthesis processes of samples were shown in Fig. 1.
## 2.3. Characterization
The morphologies and microstructures were observed by using field emission scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Hitachi S4800). The specific surface areas (Brunauer-Emmett-Teller, BET) and the mesopore size distributions (Barrett-Joyner-Halenda, BJH) were characterized by using Micromeritics ASAP 2020. The ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption curve was measured on BELSORP-max. X-ray diffraction (XRD) spectra were carried out by the Rigaku Smart Lab diffractometer. UV–vis diffused reflectance spectra (DRS) were tested on the Shimadzu UV-2450 spectrophotometer. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was measured on the Perkin Elmer PHI 5000C ESCA system. Fourier transforms infrared (FT-IR) spectra and insitu FTIR were collected on a Nicole spectrometer. Time-resolved photoluminescence (PL) spectra were measured with a Hitachi F4500 spectrometer. Time-photocurrent (TPC) measurements and electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) were collected by an electrochemical workstation (CHI-660D) with a standard threeelectrode configuration.
## 2.4. Photocatalytic activity tests
The photocatalytic activity of samples was evaluated within a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ reactor with a quartz window. The reactor was operated under a Xenon arc lamp (300 W, $400\ \mathrm{nm}<\lambda<780\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Typically, $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of samples were added to distilled water $20~\mathrm{mL}$ and then sealed. Subsequently, the reactor was filled with high-purity ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ $(99.999\mathrm{~\%~})$ several times for removing the air as far as possible. After the reaction, $2{\mathrm{~mL~}}$ suspension was collected and analyzed by gas chromatography (GC-8890) equipped with a headspace autosampler (HS-7890), a flame ionization detector (FID), and a capillary column (PEG-20 M) [30].
## 2.5. Density-Functional theory (DFT) calculations
The work functions and charge difference density plot of samples were calculated on CASTEP with a generalized gradient approximation (GGA) Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) method [31]. According to the results of experiments, the $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ models were established by exposing the (100) and (222) crystal planes, respectively. The model of $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ with (1 0 0) surface and $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ with (2 2 2) surface were all built on $2\times2$ supercells. For the models of $\mathsf{C N/I N O}$ , the mismatch rate of the model was controlled within $5\%$ to ensure the reasonableness of model construction. The cut-off energy of plane-wave basis kinetic was set at $600~\mathrm{eV}$ , the convergence tolerance was set as $1~\times~10^{-5}$ $\mathrm{eV}/{\mathring{\mathrm{A}}},$ and the k point was set as $1\times1\times1$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of samples
Micromorphology and microstructure of samples were observed by SEM. As depicted in Fig. 2a-d, the BCN exhibited a chunky agglomerated structure, and the micromorphology of InMIL-68 exhibited a slender hexagonal rod-like structure. After annealing, the rod-like structure of In-MIL-68 transformed into a hollow hexagonal microtube structure of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ (Fig. 2e and f). The length of the hexagonal tubular was about $10~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , and the diameter of the hexagonal tube was about $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ . As shown in Fig. $2\mathrm{g}$ and h, the CN/INO-3 presented the mixing of tubular and block due to the excessive amount of BCN. From Fig. 2i and j, it was obvious that the ends of the hexagonal microtube were sealed with CN/INO-2, and the walls of the tube were covered with $g_{-}$ $\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ . The result indicated that $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ was fully encapsulated on hexagonal microtube $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . When the amount of $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ was reduced, the morphology of samples (Fig. 2k and l) was still dominated by hollow hexagonal tubular.
The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image (Fig. 3a) showed the hollow hexagonal tubular structure of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ consisting of nanocrystals. High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) images (Fig. 3b) showed the lattice spacing of 0.285 and $0.402\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the (222) and (21 1) crystal surfaces of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ [32,33]. As shown in the TEM image of CN/INO-2 (Fig. 3d), hollow hexagonal tubular $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ was completely coated with $g_{-}$ $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ . From the HRTEM image (Fig. 3e) of CN/INO-2, it could be observed that the (22 2) crystal surface of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ formed a heterogeneous junction structure with $g{\mathrm{-}}C_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ . As depicted in the selected area electron diffraction (SAED) images (Fig. 3c and f) of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ and CN/INO-2, the halos corresponded to the (211), (222), (4 0 0), (3 3 2), (4 3 1), (4 4 0), and (6 2 2) surface of samples, respectively [34,35]. In the selected area electron diffraction (SAED) image, the brightest raster corresponded to the (222) surface which was the main exposing plane of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and CN/INO-2. The brightness of the grating corresponding to the other crystal surface was reduced which was caused by the enwrapping of $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ on the $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ hollow hexagonal tubular. In addition, the diameter of the grating also changed which was consistent with the change in lattice stripe spacing. EDS elemental mapping (Fig. 3g-l) revealed the existence of C, N, O, and In elements in the heterostructure. These elements were uniformly distributed on the heterojunction, further evidencing the formation of heterojunction.
The crystal structure of samples was determined by XRD. As depicted in Fig. 4a, two distinctive characteristic diffraction peaks were present in the XRD patterns of the BCN samples. Two distinctive characteristic diffraction peaks could be observed at $13.3^{\circ}$ (100) and $27.6^{\circ}$ (0 0 2) which were ascribed to interlayer structural packing motif and interlayer stacking of conjugated aromatic segments, respectively [36,37]. While the sample of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ exhibited several manifest characteristic diffraction peaks which could be observed at $21.6^{\circ}$ (211), $30.8^{\circ}$ (222), $35.5^{\circ}$ (400), $51.0^{\circ}$ (440), and $60.9^{\circ}$ (622), respectively [38]. With the increase in carbon nitride dosage, the diffraction peak intensity of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ decreased gradually. Although the intensity of the diffraction peaks of $g_{-}$ $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ was not pronounced in the XRD pattern, the characteristic peaks of $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ were clearly in FT-IR analysis (Fig. 4b). As the amount of carbon nitride increased, the peak around $1600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponded to the characteristic peak of $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ became sharper (Fig. S1a) indicating the successful loading of carbon nitride nanosheets on the surface of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ hollow hexagonal tubular [39,40]. On the contrary, the peak around $600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ became weaker (Fig. S1b) which further demonstrated the enwrapping of $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ on the $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ hollow hexagonal tubular.
To further investigate the changes in the bond structure of samples, the survey and high-resolution spectra of XPS were performed. Survey XPS information of BCN, $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ , and CN/INO-2 was shown in Fig. S2. The survey XPS of CN/INO-2 mainly contained the elements In, O, C, and N. The high-resolution spectra of C 1 s were shown in Fig. 5a. The peak of BCN and CN/INO-2 remained the same. The C 1 s peak of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ was mainly derived from the adsorption of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ which from the air (Table. S1). As shown in Fig. 5b, the high-resolution O 1 s spectra of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ exhibited two main peaks at 529.6 and $531.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ which correspond to the lattice oxygen and oxygen vacancies (Table. S2) [41,42]. Compared to $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ , the binding energy of CN/INO-2 with lattice oxygen and oxygen vacancies shifted up to a higher energy position, indicating the oxygen elements in CN/INO-2 interacted with other atoms [43– 45]. Moreover, the binding energy of In 3d (Fig. 5c) exhibited a similar phenomenon in which the In 3d peaks of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ from 444.2 and 451.7 eV up shifted to the 444.7 and $452.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ of CN/INO-2 (Table. S3). Likewise, an upward shift occurred in the binding energy of N 1 s (Fig. 5d). The peak area ratio of N 1 s changed (Table. S4) significantly suggesting the nitride atoms interacted with the other elements. The above changes in XPS further indicated the formation of heterojunction between $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ in the sample of CN/INO2.
The BET surface areas of samples were performed by $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption analysis. As described in Fig. 6, all the samples depicted a type-IV isotherm with a hysteresis loop in the range of $0.6\mathrm{-}1.0P/P_{0}$ , suggesting the existence of mesopores [46]. The surface area of BCN only exhibited $4.6\ m^{2}{\cdot}{\bf g}^{-1}$ , while the hollow hexagonal microtube $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ exhibited $30.7~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Surprisingly, the CN/INO-3, CN/INO-2, and CN/INO-1 exhibited a higher surface area of about 61.3, 56.0, and $61.7\:\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The specific surface areas of the composite samples were all higher than those of the single samples which might be caused by the interaction of the BCN with the organic ligand in the In-MIL-68. As described in the pore size distribution diagram, it could be observed that the pore size of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ was mainly distributed around $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ . However, the part pore size of CN/INO mainly converted to $3\mathrm{nm}$ and the part pore size of $\mathsf{C N/I N O}$ distributed from $20\mathrm{nm}$ to $50\mathrm{nm}$ which further demonstrated the interaction between the ligan of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and the gas of BCN pyrolysis. The gas from the BCN might inhibit the formation of pores on the MOFs. In addition, the enwrapping of g$\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ nanosheets on MOFs might form new stacking holes.
## 3.2. Photocatalytic $C O_{2}$ reduction
The photocatalytic performance of samples was appraised by photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction at ambient temperature and pressure. The gas products were detected by gas chromatography equipped with a nickel conversion furnace. The liquid products were detected by gas chromatography with a headspace sampler. As shown in (Fig. 7a), the yield of CO product from ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction with CN/INO-2 could reach $7.94~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ which was much higher than BCN and $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ . While the yield of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ product could reach $1.44~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ which was much higher than BCN and $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ . In addition, the yield of CO product from ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction with ${\mathsf{C N/I N O}}–1$ and ${\mathsf{C N/I N O}}–3$ was 6.33 and $3.74\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ , respectively. While the yield of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ product was 0.98 and $2.41~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ , respectively. The manifestly augmented photocatalytic performance was ascribed to the fabulously improved surface area, enhanced charge transfer capability, and enlarged hydrogen proton driving force. Furthermore, the control experiments (Fig. 7b) were operated under different conditions to confirm the methanol produced from ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . No methanol was detected without light or catalysts. Likewise, with the replacement of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ , no methanol was detected. Moreover, the catalyst maintained good performance after 4 rounds of the recycling test (Fig. 7c). Meanwhile, the XRD image (Fig. 7d) spectra of CN/INO2 remained almost unchanged after the reaction, evidencing its excellent photostability. Meanwhile, the FTIR of the catalyst (Fig. S3) exhibited similar characteristic peaks before and after reaction. The above results further suggested the structural stability of the catalyst.
## 3.3. The investigation of the photocatalytic mechanism
To investigate the original factors of augmented photocatalytic activity, the BCN, $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ , and CN/INO-2 were characterized by transient photocurrent and EIS analyses. Compared to BCN and $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ the CN/INO-2 exhibited higher transient photocurrent density (Fig. 8a) indicating more photogenerated carriers after being excited by light. Moreover, the CN/INO-2 exhibited a smaller EIS Nyquist plot semicircle (Fig. 8b) suggesting the charge transfer resistance was significantly reduced within a heterogeneous junction. To further understand the origin of excellent performance, the PL spectrum (Fig. 8c) and time-resolved photoluminescence (Fig. 8d) were tested. The PL intensity of CN/INO-2 was distinctly strengthened, revealing plenty of photogenerated electrons combined with holes [47,48]. In detail, the PL lifetimes of BCN, $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ and CN/INO-2 were 9.69, 25.43, and 1.79 ns, respectively. The increased recombination of photogenerated carriers indicated rapid charge migration through heterojunction [49]. What is more, the CN/INO-2 exhibited shortened $\tau_{1},\tau_{2}$ and $\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}$ of PL lifetimes, indicating the heterogeneous junction structure could accelerate the transfer of photogenerated carriers [50]. The above results demonstrated that the surface heterojunction played an important role in the migration of photogenerated carriers.
To further determined the type of heterojunction, the valence band energies $(\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{VB}})$ of samples were determined by the XPS valence band spectra (Fig. 9a). The $\tt E_{V B}$ of BCN, $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , and $\mathsf{C N}/$ INO-2 were determined to be 2.18, 1.96, and $2.25\ \mathrm{eV}$ (vs NHE), respectively. Meanwhile, the band gap $(\mathtt{E_{g}})$ of BCN, $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ , and CN/INO-2 were determined to be 2.70, 2.86, and $2.96~\mathrm{eV}$ by Tauc plots (Fig. 9b). To clarify the charge transfer path situation in the heterojunction, the work functions $(\Phi)$ of BCN and $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ were calculated by DFT. To better match the characteristics of the material itself, the surfaces of BCN (1 0 0) and $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ (222) were constructed according to the previous results of HRTEM. As shown in Fig. 9c and d, the work functions of BCN and $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ were calculated to be 4.57 and $5.58\ \mathrm{\eV}$ , respectively. In general, when two different materials were constructed to a heterojunction, the energy band bending occurred at the interfaces [51,52]. Meanwhile, the carriers would transfer from higher energy levels to lower energy levels. Therefore, the photogenerated carriers would transfer from the (222) surface of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ to the (100) surface of BCN. The result was further proved by the charge difference density plot (Fig. S4) which could display the situation of charge transfer. In the diagram of the charge difference density plot, the blue and yellow sections represented the gaining and loss region of electrons, respectively. Apparently, the loss region of the electron was mainly distributed on the BCN. At the same time, the electrons lost from BCN were transferred to $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ .
To get insight into the path of charge transfer, the Kelvin Probe Force Microscopy (KPFM), shown in Fig. 10a-d, was measured to further demonstrate the results of DFT. In the surface potential distribution with a dark environment, the difference in potential between the sample surface and substrate surface was $51.87~\mathrm{mV}.$ While the sample was exposed to visible light, the surface potential of the whole area decreased significantly which indicated the accumulation of photogenerated electrons and holes. At the same time, the difference in potential between the sample surface and substrate surface decreased to $50.57~\mathrm{mV}$ This change was proof of the photogenerated electrons transferred from the surface $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ to the internal of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ suggesting that the Z-scheme system of photogenerated carriers transfer mechanism within the sample [53,54]. Therefore, the possible band alignment and electron transfer path of the Z-scheme structure was determined in Fig. 10e and f.
For deep insights into the reaction pathway of photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction over CN/INO-2, the in-situ FTIR (Fig. 11a and d) were measured to track the intermediates during the process of photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction. As shown in Fig. 11b, the peaks at around $3645{-}3305~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were attributed to absorbed $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ . the origin of peaks at around $3645{-}3305~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ exhibited weak and broad bands of the gas-phase $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ molecular. With the increase of light duration, the peaks at around $3645{-}3305~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ started to split and shifted to higher wavenumbers indicating $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ molecules dissociate and provide more protons to participate in the photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction. Meanwhile, the peaks at $1642~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Fig. 11c) were assigned to ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{-}$ , which was converted from the absorbed ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecules [55]. Especially, the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{-}$ species were an important indicator for more efficient activation of the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ on CN/INO-2. The dynamic spectra exhibited peaks at 1308, 1372, and $1455~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ on CN/INO-2 could be assigned to the C-OH mode in bicarbonates $(\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-})$ . Generally, the protonation of carbonate species facilitated the production of abundant $\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}[$ [56].
Based on the experimental and theoretical calculation results, the possible reaction of the photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction mechanism was proposed in Fig. 12. Firstly, the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were absorbed on the surface of CN/INO-2. Compared to single sample, the composites exhibited higher specific surface area which could provide more active site for photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . More importantly, the fully encapsulated hollow tubular structure of composites could supply an internal space and enhance light collection to promote ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction. When the light illuminated on the CN/INO-2, the surficial $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ produced plenty of photogenerated electrons and migrated to the internal $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . Due to the large number of electrons, the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ in the hexagonal tubular chambers could be activated to be ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{-}$ and $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ species by CN/INO-2. With the dissociation of $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ molecules into $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ species, more hydrogen protons were released to participate in the reaction. The $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ - species reacted with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{-}$ to form $C00\mathrm{{H}^{-}}$ species which could further convert to $C0^{-}$ species. Subsequently, the $C0^{-}$ species gradually converted to CO molecular. On the other hand, the hydrogen protons reacted with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{-}$ to form ${\sf H C O}_{3}^{-}$ species which finally converted to $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ . Eventually, the CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ overflowed from the hexagonal tubular chamber of CN/INO-2.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we have successfully fabricated the Z-scheme $\mathsf{C N}/$ INO heterojunction via MOFs-assisted thermal deposition. The Zscheme heterojunction exhibited a fully encapsulated hollow tubular structure which was realized by $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets wrapping on hollow hexagonal $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ tubular. The formation of CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ - OH over the optimized CN/INO-2 catalyst was 7.94 and $1.44~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ , respectively. The photocatalytic activity was significantly higher than that of BCN and $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\cal O}_{3}$ . DFT and KPFM further demonstrated the carriers moved from $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ to $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ indicating the hollow tubular structure of $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ provided internal space for the reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ inside the catalyst. In addition, the unique wrapping structure exhibited robust light-harvesting ability for promoting the reduction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ . Based on the above, the optimized CN/INO-2 catalysts exhibited superior performance on photocatalytic $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction. This work provided a novel perspective for designing MOFs-assisted photocatalysts toward ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion.
